# Shell scripts MUST check out with LF, on every platform.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites every text file
# to CRLF on checkout. A CRLF `install.sh` is not a slightly-odd script: bash
# reads the trailing \r as part of the command and the very first line dies with
# `$'\r': command not found`. The documented one-line install
# (`git clone … && cd Ava/deploy && ./install.sh`) runs fine in Git Bash, which
# ships with Git for Windows — but only if the checkout did not corrupt it first.
#
# This is repo-wide, not deploy-only: agent-entrypoint.sh and the qa/ scripts are
# executed the same way and break the same way.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning inverted, and it is not cosmetic. cmd.exe parses a batch file
# line by line as it executes it, and LF-only endings make it mis-read labels and
# `goto` targets — deploy/install.cmd would fail in ways that look like a broken
# installer rather than a broken checkout. These are only ever run on Windows.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
